{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/panel-extraction-and-dimension-check.md", "content": "# Panel extraction and output-dimension validation

When demonstrations have unusually wide/tall inputs and much smaller outputs, inspect the entire layout before assuming a cellwise transformation. Look for a separator line/column and distinct panels on either side. Across demos, compare output dimensions to the panel dimensions: if the output consistently matches one panel, the task likely extracts or transforms that panel while discarding the other, separator, or framing. Normalize colors by role and test candidate panel selection against every example.

After inferring the selected panel, determine its transformation independently from the discarded region. Do not let random-looking cells elsewhere in the input distract from a compact output panel. Apply the same spatial crop/projection to the test input, then map colors using the demos. Validate the resulting row count and each row width against the inferred output dimensions before emitting JSON. Incorrect feedback may stem from a wrong selection rule, a wrong transformation, or simply malformed output dimensions; distinguish these failure classes before spending another attempt.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/panel-extraction-and-dimension-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint", "file": "references/panel-exception-lesson.md", "content": "# Panel classification: exceptions and post-rejection audit

In separator-panel tasks, correct panel dimensions do not validate a marker-presence label rule. Across every demonstration, make a full table of panels and outputs, recording marker presence, count, and arrangement. A marked panel may share the background output label while another marked panel receives the special label; train the signature against all examples rather than extrapolating from early uniformly labeled demos.

For a rejected output, treat the whole mapping as suspect. Recheck panel extraction and reading order, then revisit what feature distinguishes labels. Do not merely edit a few cells based on binary feedback. Request another example only if surviving signatures predict different useful outcomes and the cost is justified; otherwise apply the strongest candidate consistently to all test panels.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-evidence-checkpoint/references/panel-exception-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/failed-hypothesis-revision.md", "content": "# Revising hypotheses after incorrect submissions

A wrong grid rejects the entire submitted hypothesis, but binary feedback does not locate the error. Do not respond by shifting the same speculative recoloring boundary or trying arbitrary minor variants. First compare every demo input/output cellwise and list the precise changed coordinates, including isolated endpoint changes and preserved lines. Distinguish object-internal changes from continuation/tail changes, and test predictions against all examples.

Request another demo only when you can state which live candidate rules it could separate. Otherwise derive a materially different rule from the examples and apply it once, preserving all cells not selected by that rule. This discipline prevents a sequence of unsupported guesses from spending the attempt budget.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/failed-hypothesis-revision.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/stripe-tail-mapping.md", "content": "# Mapping a recolored stripe tail from a demo

When a demo contains a distinctive irregular stripe followed by a contiguous tail of cells recolored to another value, identify the exact unchanged motif and changed tail cell by cell. In the observed example, a color-7 stripe continued into a tail of color 1 through the right edge; the irregular color-7 portion remained intact. On a test instance, locate the corresponding motif and recolor only its continuation, matching the same direction and endpoint. Leave unrelated stripes and background untouched. This was validated by a correct submission in the session that produced this note; do not generalize the specific colors or rightward direction without checking the current demo.
